DETECTION OPPORTUNITIES

Without initial-access evidence, focus on post-compromise behavior.

Identity

Look for:

first-seen device
impossible/unusual travel
new authentication method
MFA reset
unusual privileged account usage
abnormal OAuth grants
new service credentials
unusual token refresh patterns
Corporate Data

Look for:

bulk patient-record access
unusually broad queries
file enumeration
archive creation
high-volume downloads
cross-business-unit access
abnormal database exports
Egress

Look for:

high-volume outbound traffic
new destinations
encrypted outbound sessions outside baseline
cloud-storage upload tools
unusual compression
scripted exfiltration
DETECTION MODEL

Conceptually:

NEW IDENTITY SESSION
        ↓
BROAD DATA DISCOVERY
        ↓
PATIENT DATA ACCESS
        ↓
ARCHIVE CREATION
        ↓
LARGE OUTBOUND TRANSFER

Any one event may look harmless.

Together they are a breach story.

CROSS-DOMAIN DETECTION

Because Medtronic has multiple security domains, detection should also watch boundary crossing.

Example:

CORPORATE IDENTITY
      ↓
ATTEMPTS PRODUCT ACCESS
      ↓
CRITICAL ALERT

or:

CORP DEVICE
    ↓
MANUFACTURING MANAGEMENT PLANE
    ↓
CRITICAL ALERT

Segmentation shouldn't merely block traffic.

Attempted boundary crossing should create telemetry.

ATTACK BREAKERS
Breaker 1 — Separate corporate and product identities

Not merely separate networks.

Breaker 2 — Separate privileged administration

No universal admin plane.

Breaker 3 — Product-network allowlisting

Corporate systems should not have arbitrary access.

Breaker 4 — Separate PKI / certificates

Don't let corporate credential compromise become product trust.

Breaker 5 — Separate backups

Corporate ransomware should not reach product/manufacturing recovery systems.

Breaker 6 — Patient-data minimization

Corporate systems should retain only what is required.

Breaker 7 — Bulk-access detection

Millions of patient identities should never be quietly enumerable.

Breaker 8 — DLP / egress monitoring

Patient information leaving expected workflows should be high-severity.

Breaker 9 — Independent security logging

Attackers shouldn't be able to erase both corporate activity and boundary evidence.

Breaker 10 — Regular segmentation testing

Not:

“Firewall rule exists.”

Actually attempt compromise traversal.

