THE INFINITYGRID TAKEAWAY

Medtronic demonstrates something the security industry often forgets:

A cybersecurity incident can be enormous without becoming a safety incident.

Millions of people's information can be exposed while:

devices continue operating
manufacturing continues
products remain available
hospitals stay connected
patient therapies continue

That doesn't make the breach minor.

It means segmentation may have prevented a different and potentially far more dangerous category of consequence.

The right lesson is not:

“Nothing happened to the devices, so the security worked.”

Nor is it:

“Millions of records were exposed, so everything failed.”

The mature conclusion is:

Some controls appear to have failed catastrophically around confidentiality while other architectural boundaries appear to have held.

Security teams need to study both.

MEDTRONIC: 3.8 MILLION PEOPLE EXPOSED — BUT THE ATTACK STOPPED BEFORE THE MEDICAL DEVICES. WHY?

That is the architectural question worth answering.

INFINITYGRID PRINCIPLE

The measure of segmentation is not whether two networks have different IP ranges. It is whether compromise of one can become compromise of the other.

And:

A control that contains the blast radius deserves the same forensic attention as the control that failed.
